# Partita No. 3 in A minor, BWV 827: Fantasia (J. S. Bach)
#
# Identification: Mutopia's BWV-827.mid is the first score of the
# combined BWV-827.ly, the same music as BWV-827-1.ly, headed
# "1. Prelude": 118 bars of 3/8 in A minor, a two-part invention. It is
# the Fantasia: Bach titled it Prelude in the 1725 Anna Magdalena
# notebook version and Fantasia in the Clavierubung I print (1727/1731),
# so "Fantasia" is the right title for BWV 827.
#
# Character: a two-voice invention in quick 3/8, one beat to the bar,
# in continuous sixteenths. The subject is a bar of turning sixteenths
# (rest, A B G# A C | D B E D C B) answered at the octave; its
# countersubject is a rising line of eighths tied over the bar (C E A~,
# A G# B~). Bright, restless and driving, with chromatic darkening in
# the middle.
#
# Time signature: 3/8, 118 bars in the Mutopia score and in the MIDI,
# no upbeat, no repeats (the Fantasia has none); fermata on the last
# chord.
#
# Tempo: dotted quarter (one bar) = 52, about 2:20.
# Track times: Angela Hewitt (Hyperion, 2018) 1:58, about 60 bars a
# minute; Richard Egarr (harpsichord, Harmonia Mundi) 2:35, about 46;
# Jory Vinikour (harpsichord, Sono Luminus) 2:45, about 43. 52 sits
# between Hewitt and the harpsichordists: sixteenths about 11.5 frames
# each, unhurried enough for the two lines to stay distinct on the
# chip.
#
# Structure (my reading of the Mutopia score):
#   1-2     subject, top, A minor, over the bass's A octaves.
#   3-4     subject, bass, at the octave; countersubject above.
#   5-16    sequences on the subject and countersubject; cadence in A
#           minor (16-17).
#   17-30   episode: the top's falling figure over a bass stepping down
#           in octaves (21-23), then broken chords falling by fifths,
#           toward E minor.
#   31-34   subject, bass, E minor (31), then top (33).
#   35-50   sequences in E minor and G; cadence in E minor (47-50).
#   50-61   a long held F sharp (54) and descending sequences.
#   62-66   subject, bass, E minor again; cadence (65-66).
#   66-78   chromatic modulation: B flat and C sharp, D minor (70),
#           then back to A minor; cadence on E at 78.
#   79-96   the episode of 21 returns in A minor, over the stepping bass.
#   97-103  the opening's bass and figures return in A minor.
#   104-110 long held dissonances (F, G sharp, B), then the parallel
#           sequences of 9-14 again.
#   111-118 the close; final cadence in A minor, fermata.
#
# Plan: terraced by section, as on a two-manual harpsichord: the
# exposition mf, the first episode a step down, the E minor subject
# entries full, the chromatic middle soft and building, the reprise of
# the episode mf, and the return of the opening material full to the
# end. Subject entries in the bass brought forward.

tempo 52 4.

dyn 1 mf           # 1-16: exposition, A minor
lead 3-4 bottom    # subject, bass, at the octave
broaden 16 0.95    # the A minor cadence
dyn 17 mp          # 17-30: the first episode, a softer manual
dyn 31 f           # 31-49: the subject in E minor, full
lead 31-32 bottom  # subject, bass, E minor
lead 33-34 top     # subject, top
broaden 49 0.95    # the E minor cadence
dyn 50 mf          # 50-65: the second E minor section
lead 62-63 bottom  # subject, bass, E minor again
dyn 66 mp          # 66-78: the chromatic middle, soft
ramp 74-78 mf      # building back to A minor
broaden 78 0.94    # the cadence on E before the reprise
dyn 79 mf          # 79-96: the episode returns in A minor
dyn 97 f           # 97-118: the opening material returns, full

rit 4 0.7          # the last four (short) bars into the fermata
